      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwe first need to recognize the standard form of the hyperbola. The standard form for a hyperbola that opens left and right is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nBy comparing the given equation with the standard form, we can identify that \\(a^2 = 99\\) and \\(b^2 = 36\\). Therefore, we have\n\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{99} = 3\\sqrt{11} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\n\\]\n\nFor a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the distance between the vertices is given by \\(2a\\). Thus, we calculate \\(2a\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\cdot 3\\sqrt{11} = 6\\sqrt{11}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{11}}.\n\\]",
